.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OSSL_HTTP_TRANSFER 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_open ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_proxy_connect ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_set1_request ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_exchange ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_get ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_transfer ,
.Nm OSSL_HTTP_close
.Nd HTTP client high-level functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/http.h
.Ft typedef BIO *
.Fo 
.Fa *OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t
.Fc
(BIO *bio, void *arg,
.Bd -literal
                                    int connect, int detail);
 OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *OSSL_HTTP_open(const char *server, const char *port,
                                   const char *proxy, const char *no_proxy,
                                   int use_ssl, BIO *bio, BIO *rbio,
                                   OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t bio_update_fn, void *arg,
                                   int buf_size, int overall_timeout);
 int OSSL_HTTP_proxy_connect(BIO *bio, const char *server, const char *port,
                             const char *proxyuser, const char *proxypass,
                             int timeout, BIO *bio_err, const char *prog);
 int OSSL_HTTP_set1_request(OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *rctx, const char *path,
                            const STACK_OF(CONF_VALUE) *headers,
                            const char *content_type, BIO *req,
                            const char *expected_content_type, int expect_asn1,
                            size_t max_resp_len, int timeout, int keep_alive);
 BIO *OSSL_HTTP_exchange(OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *rctx, char **redirection_url);
 BIO *OSSL_HTTP_get(const char *url, const char *proxy, const char *no_proxy,
                    BIO *bio, BIO *rbio,
                    OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t bio_update_fn, void *arg,
                    int buf_size, const STACK_OF(CONF_VALUE) *headers,
                    const char *expected_content_type, int expect_asn1,
                    size_t max_resp_len, int timeout);
 BIO *OSSL_HTTP_transfer(OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX **prctx,
                         const char *server, const char *port,
                         const char *path, int use_ssl,
                         const char *proxy, const char *no_proxy,
                         BIO *bio, BIO *rbio,
                         OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t bio_update_fn, void *arg,
                         int buf_size, const STACK_OF(CONF_VALUE) *headers,
                         const char *content_type, BIO *req,
                         const char *expected_content_type, int expect_asn1,
                         size_t max_resp_len, int timeout, int keep_alive);
 int OSSL_HTTP_close(OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *rctx, int ok);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
initiates an HTTP session using the
.Em bio
argument if not NULL, else by connecting to a given
.Em server
optionally via a
.Em proxy .
.Pp
Typically the OpenSSL build supports sockets and the
.Em bio
parameter is NULL. In this case
.Em rbio
must be NULL as well and the
.Em server
must be non-NULL. The function creates a network BIO internally using
.Xr BIO_new_connect 3
for connecting to the given server and the optionally given
.Em port ,
defaulting to 80 for HTTP or 443 for HTTPS. Then this internal BIO is
used for setting up a connection and for exchanging one or more request
and response.
.Pp
If
.Em bio
is given and
.Em rbio
is NULL then this
.Em bio
is used instead.
If both
.Em bio
and
.Em rbio
are given (which may be memory BIOs for instance) then no explicit
connection is set up, but
.Em bio
is used for writing requests and
.Em rbio
for reading responses.
As soon as the client has flushed
.Em bio
the server must be ready to provide a response or indicate a waiting
condition via
.Em rbio .
.Pp
If
.Em bio
is given, it is an error to provide non-NULL
.Em proxy
or
.Em no_proxy
arguments, while
.Em server
and
.Em port
arguments may be given to support diagnostic output.
If
.Em bio
is NULL the optional
.Em proxy
parameter can be used to set an HTTP(S) proxy to use (unless overridden
by "no_proxy" settings).
If TLS is not used this defaults to the environment variable
.Qo Li http_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTP_PROXY Qc .
If
.Em use_ssl
!= 0 it defaults to
.Qo Li https_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTPS_PROXY Qc .
An empty proxy string
.Qo Li \(dq" Qc
forbids using a proxy.
Otherwise, the format is
.Qo Li [http[s]://][userinfo@]host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment] Qc ,
where any userinfo, path, query, and fragment given is ignored.
If the host string is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
The default proxy port number is 80, or 443 in case "https:" is given.
The HTTP client functions connect via the given proxy unless the
.Em server
is found in the optional list
.Em no_proxy
of proxy hostnames or IP addresses separated by
.Qo Li , Qc
and/or whitespace (if not NULL; default is the environment variable
.Qo Li no_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li NO_PROXY Qc Ns ).
Proxying plain HTTP is supported directly, while using a proxy for HTTPS
connections requires a suitable callback function such as
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_proxy_connect 3 ,
described below.
.Pp
If
.Em use_ssl
is nonzero a TLS connection is requested and the
.Em bio_update_fn
parameter must be provided.
.Pp
The parameter
.Em bio_update_fn ,
which is optional if
.Em use_ssl
is 0, may be used to modify the connection BIO used by the HTTP client,
but cannot be used when both
.Em bio
and
.Em rbio
are given.
.Em bio_update_fn
is a BIO connect/disconnect callback function with prototype
.Bd -literal
 BIO *(*OSSL_HTTP_bio_cb_t)(BIO *bio, void *arg, int connect, int detail)
.Ed
.Pp
The callback function may modify the BIO provided in the
.Em bio
argument, whereby it may use an optional custom defined argument
.Em arg ,
which can for instance point to an
.Sy SSL_CTX
structure.
During connection establishment, just after calling
.Xr BIO_do_connect_retry 3 ,
the callback function is invoked with the
.Em connect
argument being 1 and
.Em detail
being 1 if
.Em use_ssl
is nonzero (i.e., HTTPS is requested), else 0. On disconnect
.Em connect
is 0 and
.Em detail
is 1 if no error occurred, else 0. For instance, on connect the callback
may push an SSL BIO to implement HTTPS; after disconnect it may do some
diagnostic output and pop and free the SSL BIO.
.Pp
The callback function must return either the potentially modified BIO
.Em bio
or NULL to indicate failure, in which case it should not modify the BIO.
.Pp
Here is a simple example that supports TLS connections (but not via a
proxy):
.Bd -literal
 BIO *http_tls_cb(BIO *bio, void *arg, int connect, int detail)
 {
     if (connect && detail) { /* connecting with TLS */
         SSL_CTX *ctx = (SSL_CTX *)arg;
         BIO *sbio = BIO_new_ssl(ctx, 1);

         bio = sbio != NULL ? BIO_push(sbio, bio) : NULL;
     } else if (!connect) { /* disconnecting */
         BIO *hbio;

         if (!detail) { /* an error has occurred */
             /* optionally add diagnostics here */
         }
         BIO_ssl_shutdown(bio);
         hbio = BIO_pop(bio);
         BIO_free(bio); /* SSL BIO */
         bio = hbio;
     }
     return bio;
 }
.Ed
.Pp
After disconnect the modified BIO will be deallocated using
.Xr BIO_free_all 3 .
The optional callback function argument
.Em arg
is not consumed, so must be freed by the caller when not needed any
more.
.Pp
The
.Em buf_size
parameter specifies the response header maximum line length.
A value <= 0 means that the
.Sy OSSL_HTTP_DEFAULT_MAX_LINE_LEN
(4KiB) is used.
.Em buf_size
is also used as the number of content bytes that are read at a time.
.Pp
If the
.Em overall_timeout
parameter is > 0 this indicates the maximum number of seconds the
overall HTTP transfer (i.e., connection setup if needed, sending
requests, and receiving responses) is allowed to take until completion.
A value <= 0 enables waiting indefinitely, i.e., no timeout.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_proxy_connect 3
may be used by an above BIO connect callback function to set up an
SSL/TLS connection via an HTTPS proxy.
It promotes the given BIO
.Em bio
representing a connection pre-established with a TLS proxy using the
HTTP CONNECT method, optionally using proxy client credentials
.Em proxyuser
and
.Em proxypass ,
to connect with TLS protection ultimately to
.Em server
and
.Em port .
If the
.Em port
argument is NULL or the empty string it defaults to "443". If the
.Em timeout
parameter is > 0 this indicates the maximum number of seconds the
connection setup is allowed to take.
A value <= 0 enables waiting indefinitely, i.e., no timeout.
Since this function is typically called by applications such as
.Xr openssl-s_client 1
it uses the
.Em bio_err
and
.Em prog
parameters (unless NULL) to print additional diagnostic information in a
user-oriented way.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3
sets up in
.Em rctx
the request header and content data and expectations on the response
using the following parameters.
If <rctx> indicates using a proxy for HTTP (but not HTTPS), the server
host (and optionally port) needs to be placed in the header; thus it
must be present in
.Em rctx .
For backward compatibility, the server (and optional port) may also be
given in the
.Em path
argument beginning with
.Qo Li http:// Qc
(thus giving an absoluteURI). If
.Em path
is NULL it defaults to "/". If
.Em req
is NULL the HTTP GET method will be used to send the request else HTTP
POST with the contents of
.Em req
and optional
.Em content_type ,
where the length of the data in
.Em req
does not need to be determined in advance: the BIO will be read
on-the-fly while sending the request, which supports streaming.
The optional list
.Em headers
may contain additional custom HTTP header lines.
The
.Em max_resp_len
parameter specifies the maximum allowed response content length, where
the value 0 indicates no limit.
For the meaning of the
.Em expected_content_type ,
.Em expect_asn1 ,
.Em timeout ,
and
.Em keep_alive
parameters, see
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_expected 3 .
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_exchange 3
exchanges any form of HTTP request and response as specified by
.Em rctx ,
which must include both connection and request data, typically set up
using
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3 .
It implements the core of the functions described below.
If the HTTP method is GET and
.Em redirection_url
is not NULL the latter pointer is used to provide any new location that
the server may return with HTTP code 301 (MOVED_PERMANENTLY) or 302
(FOUND). In this case the function returns NULL and the caller is
responsible for deallocating the URL with
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
If the response header contains one or more
.Qo Li Content-Length Qc
lines and/or an ASN.1-encoded response is expected, which should include
a total length, the length indications received are checked for
consistency and for not exceeding any given maximum response length.
If an ASN.1-encoded response is expected, the function returns on
success the contents buffered in a memory BIO, which does not support
streaming.
Otherwise it returns directly the read BIO that holds the response
contents, which allows a response of indefinite length and may support
streaming.
The caller is responsible for freeing the BIO pointer obtained.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_get 3
uses HTTP GET to obtain data from
.Em bio
if non-NULL, else from the server contained in the
.Em url ,
and returns it as a BIO. It supports redirection via HTTP status code
301 or 302. It is meant for transfers with a single round trip, so does
not support persistent connections.
If
.Em bio
is non-NULL, any host and port components in the
.Em url
are not used for connecting but the hostname is used, as usual, for the
.Qo Li Host Qc
header.
Any userinfo and fragment components in the
.Em url
are ignored.
Any query component is handled as part of the path component.
If the scheme component of the
.Em url
is
.Qo Li https Qc
a TLS connection is requested and the
.Em bio_update_fn ,
as described for
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3 ,
must be provided.
Also the remaining parameters are interpreted as described for
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3 ,
respectively.
The caller is responsible for freeing the BIO pointer obtained.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3
exchanges an HTTP request and response over a connection managed via
.Em prctx
without supporting redirection.
It combines
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_exchange 3 ,
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_close 3 .
If
.Em prctx
is not NULL it reuses any open connection represented by a non-NULL
.Em *prctx .
It keeps the connection open if a persistent connection is requested or
required and this was granted by the server, else it closes the
connection and assigns NULL to
.Em *prctx .
The remaining parameters are interpreted as described for
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3 ,
respectively.
The caller is responsible for freeing the BIO pointer obtained.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_close 3
closes the connection and releases
.Em rctx .
The
.Em ok
parameter is passed to any BIO update function given during setup as
described above for
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3 .
It must be 1 if no error occurred during the HTTP transfer and 0
otherwise.
.Sh NOTES
The names of the environment variables used by this implementation:
.Qo Li http_proxy Qc ,
.Qo Li HTTP_PROXY Qc ,
.Qo Li https_proxy Qc ,
.Qo Li HTTPS_PROXY Qc ,
.Qo Li no_proxy Qc ,
and
.Qo Li NO_PROXY Qc ,
have been chosen for maximal compatibility with other HTTP client
implementations such as wget, curl, and git.
.Pp
When built with tracing enabled,
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3
and all functions using it may be traced using
.Sy OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_HTTP .
See also
.Xr OSSL_trace_enabled 3
and
.Xr openssl-env 7 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
returns on success a
.Sy OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX ,
else NULL.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_proxy_connect 3
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_set1_request 3
return 1 on success, 0 on error.
.Pp
On success,
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_exchange 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_get 3 ,
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3
return a memory BIO that buffers all the data received if an
ASN.1-encoded response is expected, otherwise a BIO that may support
streaming.
The BIO must be freed by the caller.
On failure, they return NULL. Failure conditions include
connection/transfer timeout, parse errors, etc.
The caller is responsible for freeing the BIO pointer obtained.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_close 3
returns 0 if anything went wrong while disconnecting, else 1.
